export const guides = [
  {joint:'dado',slug:'measure-plywood-for-a-dado',title:'Measure Plywood Before Sizing a Dado',description:'Build a stock-thickness record, separate measured variation from fit allowance, and test a housing before cutting cabinet sides.',intro:'The thickness written on a sheet label is a purchasing description. A housing needs the thickness of the particular part that will enter it.',worked:'A shelf measures 17.8 mm at its entering edge. With an illustrative total allowance of 0.15 mm, the model produces a 17.95 mm housing. A 6 mm recess in an 18 mm side leaves a 12 mm floor. The 0.15 mm belongs to this example; it is not a recommended universal fit.',sections:[
    ['Measure the edge that enters the joint','Identify the shelf, mark its entering edge and keep its orientation with the side panel. Take readings near both ends and the middle of that edge. Record the spread as well as the largest reading. Measuring a spare strip from another sheet can miss the variation that actually determines whether this shelf seats. Keep veneer damage and raised fibers out of the measurement.'],
    ['Separate stock variation from fit allowance','Enter measured mating thickness as the stock value. Enter a separate total width allowance, which is added once across the housing. Do not quietly add the same number on both sides. If the edge varies enough that one housing cannot meet your chosen fit over its length, stop and resolve the part preparation rather than hiding the spread inside a generous allowance.'],
    ['Make a representative coupon','Use a host offcut and a mating strip from the production material, with the same reference faces. Mark the planned recess depth and record the resulting floor. Produce the trial using the intended setup and its documented method. Check seating at several points without forcing the strip. A caliper reading and an actual seating trial answer different questions; retain both in the record.'],
    ['Freeze the setup before the batch','Label the accepted coupon with stock identifiers, dimensions, date and revision. Recheck it after a cutter, fence, guide or material change. Export the dimension CSV with the part list, and keep a JSON backup of the editable record. If the production sheet differs from the coupon, revise the layout and repeat the trial before releasing the remaining sides.']
  ],checks:['Readings from the actual entering edge are recorded.','Total allowance is entered once.','The coupon and production stock share the setup.'],stop:'Stop if the shelf binds along part of the housing, the veneer is damaged, or the thickness spread has no accepted fit solution.',faq:['Should I enter 18 mm for nominal 18 mm plywood?','Enter the measured mating edge. The label can remain in your material notes, but the calculator uses the entered number literally. A 17.8 mm part and an 18 mm part produce different widths.'],sources:[0]},
  {joint:'dado',slug:'opposing-dado-datums',title:'Keep Opposing Dados on One Datum',description:'Map a matched pair of cabinet housings from finished edges and distinguish the near wall, far wall and shelf elevation.',intro:'Two individually accurate housings can still miss each other when their measurements begin at different physical edges.',worked:'The near wall is 40 mm from a marked bottom edge and the housing is 17.95 mm wide. Its far wall is therefore 57.95 mm from that same datum. Measure both sides from their corresponding bottoms. Turning one panel around does not make its opposite edge a valid substitute datum.',sections:[
    ['Name the physical references','Mark inside faces, finished bottoms and finished fronts on both side panels before drawing a housing. Write which wall defines the shelf elevation in the project notes. A centerline, lower wall and upper wall are different references. Choose one convention and use it in the cut list, layout drawing and inspection record so a later reader does not have to infer it.'],
    ['Read coordinates in the panel frame','The planner reports the near wall and far wall along a single layout axis. On a vertical side panel, that axis might run upward from the bottom. On a loose bench drawing it may appear horizontal. Follow the labels rather than the screen orientation. Transfer each coordinate from its named datum instead of measuring the second wall from a potentially imperfect first cut.'],
    ['Check the pair before fitting the shelf','Place the marked side panels in their intended assembly relationship and compare the two housing positions against a common reference. Check the host dimensions as well: matching offsets on unequal sides can still produce a case problem. Test the actual shelf in a dry assembly and observe whether both edges seat together without twisting the case or levering a side outward.'],
    ['Control later edge changes','Trimming the bottom or adding a lipping strip after layout can change what a measurement means. Record whether the datum is a raw edge, a finished edge or a planned trim line. If the physical reference moves, update the coordinates and revision. Keep the coupon and inspection note with the pair until the case has passed the full dry-fit review.']
  ],checks:['Inside, front and bottom marks are visible on both sides.','The shelf-elevation wall is named.','The assembled pair has been checked against one reference.'],stop:'Stop if the two housings use different edge conventions or if matching offsets require twisting the case to fit.',faq:['Does the diagram mirror the second cabinet side?','The housing planner describes one section and its datum. It does not generate a mirrored cabinet side. Transfer the recorded coordinates into each panel’s labeled physical frame and verify the pair.'],sources:[]},
  {joint:'dado',slug:'through-versus-stopped-housings',title:'Through and Stopped Housings Need Different End Checks',description:'Use housing-width arithmetic while separately reviewing stopped ends, cutter radius, insertion travel and visible front edges.',intro:'A width and depth calculation does not describe every end of a real housing. Stopped work needs an additional plan for how the mating part enters and finishes.',worked:'A 17.95 mm-wide, 6 mm-deep housing may fit a 17.8 mm strip in section. If the recess stops before the front, that successful section says nothing about the remaining end shape. A router-produced rounded stop and a square shelf corner need a compatible detail that this model does not calculate.',sections:[
    ['Separate the section from the end condition','Use the planner for the repeated cross section: mating thickness, total allowance, recess depth and remaining host floor. Sketch the housing length and both ends separately. Identify which end is visible, which end is open and which edge is the assembly entry. A complete drawing needs those decisions even when the width calculation is already settled.'],
    ['Resolve the actual corner shape','Document the tool and setup that will create the stopped end. The tool’s swept path can leave a radius that differs from a square drawing. Choose and detail a compatible mating corner or a separate end treatment using the applicable tool method. Do not treat a dimensionally narrow interference as extra glue space; it can keep the whole shelf from reaching the seat.'],
    ['Rehearse the insertion path','A part may fit the final position while having no feasible route into it after other panels are attached. Dry assemble in the intended order and watch the front corner, rear corner and neighboring dividers. Include any back or face frame that restricts movement. Record the order that actually works before assigning the joint to a glue-up batch.'],
    ['Keep the unmodeled detail visible','Put the stopped length, corner treatment and assembly direction in the notes or an attached shop drawing. The exported SVG is a section schematic, not a routing template. Preserve the full drawing with the CSV so a future setup does not mistake the section for a complete machine path. Recheck the detail after a change in tool diameter or shelf outline.']
  ],checks:['Both housing ends have a named condition.','Mating corner geometry is compatible.','The complete assembly has a workable insertion path.'],stop:'Stop if a corner prevents full seating, the assembly cannot enter its final position, or the stopped-end detail exists only as an assumption.',faq:['Can I use the dado planner for a stopped groove?','Its section arithmetic remains useful, but the tool models a straight through housing. You must separately resolve the stopped length, end radius, corner detail and assembly path.'],sources:[]},
  {joint:'rabbet',slug:'fit-a-back-panel-seat',title:'Fit a Back Panel to a Rabbet Seat',description:'Review a back-panel edge, its open recess and the remaining lip without confusing fit allowance with seasonal panel clearance.',intro:'A back-panel seat has several independent dimensions. The recess width fits the entering edge; the panel outline and fixing method solve different problems.',worked:'For an illustrative 6 mm entering edge and 0.15 mm total seat allowance, the recess width is 6.15 mm. With an 18 mm host and 9 mm recess depth, the remaining lip is 9 mm. In this section convention, the 6.15 mm width follows the entering edge; rotate the physical drawing deliberately to match your case.',sections:[
    ['Draw the seat in section','Sketch the mating edge inside the open recess and label the two axes. Confirm which input corresponds to recess width and which corresponds to depth in your actual orientation. Cabinet drawings use several rabbet conventions, so a familiar word alone is insufficient. The planner’s highlighted recess and dimension register define the convention used for this record.'],
    ['Measure the panel edge independently','Measure the actual edge that enters the seat, including any treatment that remains there. Enter a separate total width allowance and test it using the production material. Do not infer the back’s thickness from a decorative exposed face or a supplier’s nominal sheet category. If the edge has a tongue or reduced section, model that entering section and document the rest.'],
    ['Plan the panel outline and retention','Determine the back’s overall length and width from the assembled case and its intended detail. The seat fit does not establish a movement allowance around the entire panel. Resolve solid-wood movement, panel installation direction and mechanical retention separately. Check that planned fasteners, adjacent hardware and the remaining lip are compatible with the full design.'],
    ['Inspect the seat in the full case','Dry fit the back before committing the surrounding case. Look for debris, an uneven floor, bowed material and a diagonal mismatch. Record which surface the back actually bears against. Keep the case-square review separate from the edge-fit coupon: a small trial can prove the section while the complete back reveals an assembly geometry problem.']
  ],checks:['The width and depth axes match the physical section.','The actual entering edge has been measured.','Panel outline and retention have separate details.'],stop:'Stop if the back needs force to seat, the case changes shape to accept it, or the remaining lip conflicts with a fixing.',faq:['Does the seat allowance provide room for wood movement?','It is only an allowance across the entering edge in this section. Seasonal panel movement depends on grain, panel dimensions, moisture conditions and the retention detail. Review that separately.'],sources:[0]},
  {joint:'rabbet',slug:'rabbet-shoulder-and-case-size',title:'Track the Rabbet Shoulder Without Losing the Case Size',description:'Relate an edge recess to its shoulder coordinate and separate overall panel size from a seated mating part.',intro:'A rabbet removes material from an existing edge. Its shoulder coordinate is not automatically the finished size of the part that enters it.',worked:'A host span of 180 mm and a 6.15 mm seat place the shoulder at 173.85 mm from the opposite datum. That coordinate locates the recess wall. It does not say that a back, shelf or neighboring side should be cut to 173.85 mm; the assembly drawing must determine that part’s finished dimensions.',sections:[
    ['Choose the opposite-edge datum','Mark the host edge from which the shoulder coordinate is measured. The planner places the recess at the far edge of the entered host width. If your shop drawing measures inward from the open edge, record the seat width directly and retain the conversion between the two conventions. Avoid mixing those conventions in one dimension chain.'],
    ['Keep the dimension chain explicit','Write host width, shoulder position and recess width on one section. Verify that shoulder plus width returns the host span. This simple closure check catches a surprising number of transposed numbers and drawing revisions. Carry actual host thickness and recess depth on the perpendicular axis; they describe the remaining lip and cannot replace the shoulder measurement.'],
    ['Derive mating part size from the assembly','Draw the other end of the mating part and identify its seat, reveal or clearance. Only then derive its overall size. Two rabbets, an inset edge and an overlapping back produce different chains. The one-edge planner intentionally does not infer a case configuration from a recess dimension. Record the final cut size in Cabinet Studio or the project cut list.'],
    ['Review a revision from both directions','If the back thickness or seat allowance changes, recompute the shoulder and compare the resulting assembly detail. A wider recess moves the shoulder even when the host stays the same size. Check whether the changed shoulder affects a groove, hinge, fixing or visible reveal. Archive the old CSV and issue a clearly labeled new revision before repeating the setup.']
  ],checks:['The shoulder datum is marked.','Shoulder plus recess width equals host width.','Mating cut size comes from a complete assembly chain.'],stop:'Stop if a shop dimension could refer to either the recess width or the opposite-edge shoulder, or if the mating cut size has no complete dimension chain.',faq:['Why does the shoulder move when I change allowance?','The recess remains anchored at the far edge. Increasing its width moves the near shoulder toward the datum while the overall host span remains unchanged.'],sources:[]},
  {joint:'rabbet',slug:'rabbet-assembly-and-finish',title:'Review Rabbet Assembly Before Applying Finish',description:'Plan the insertion order, bearing surfaces, glue boundaries and finish masking of a rabbeted assembly.',intro:'An edge seat can be easy to measure and awkward to assemble. Review the real sequence before either adhesive or finish changes the available choices.',worked:'A back fits a trial rabbet with an uncoated entering edge. The case is then prefinished. If coating accumulates on the bearing wall or panel edge, the original trial no longer describes the final fit. Preserve the intended bonding surfaces and repeat the physical seating check in the state used for assembly.',sections:[
    ['Rehearse with all neighboring parts','Place the back, sides, dividers and any face frame in the intended assembly order. Note whether the panel slides from an open end or drops into an exposed seat. Confirm that the final part can still be inserted after the preceding joints close. Label the mating edges so a successful rehearsal can be repeated instead of reconstructed from memory.'],
    ['Identify bearing and bonding surfaces','Mark which surfaces locate the panel and which are intended to bond. Those roles may overlap, but they are not interchangeable. Review the adhesive manufacturer’s requirements for the actual substrates. If a coated surface lies inside a proposed glue line, resolve the product compatibility and surface preparation explicitly before relying on the earlier bare-material test.'],
    ['Map finish boundaries onto the joint','Use the finishing plan to identify surfaces that will be coated before assembly and surfaces that stay available for bonding. Avoid an ambiguous note such as finish everything first. A small annotated section can show the exact boundary, visible edge and masked contact area. Keep that section with the joinery record and inspect the dry parts after the coating step.'],
    ['Record the final dry-run state','Repeat the seating check with the actual panel and case after relevant preparation. Verify the chosen retaining detail and access to clamps or fixings. Record the adhesive, planned batch and evidence observations only after this rehearsal. Geometry edits clear the observations in the planner because the earlier coupon no longer proves the revised joint.']
  ],checks:['The insertion order works with all adjacent parts.','Bonding surfaces and finish boundaries are marked.','Final prepared parts pass the seating review.'],stop:'Stop if a finished surface unintentionally enters the glue line, the back cannot enter after case assembly, or retention access is blocked.',faq:['Can a bare-stock coupon validate a prefinished joint?','It validates that bare-stock setup. If the final surfaces differ, check the changed fit and the adhesive maker’s substrate guidance before treating the assembly as reviewed.'],sources:[1]},
  {joint:'half-lap',slug:'unequal-stock-half-laps',title:'Balance a Half-Lap in Unequal Stock',description:'Solve a pair of lap depths from actual thicknesses and an explicit finished stack instead of assuming both recesses are half depth.',intro:'Equal recess depths are a special case. Unequal members need an explicit choice about the finished stack and the faces that should align.',worked:'Member A is 18 mm thick and member B is 20 mm. For a target stack of 20 mm and an 8 mm recess in A, B needs a 10 mm recess: 18 + 20 − 20 − 8 = 10. The remaining sections are 10 mm each. The stack balances, but face offsets still need the assembly drawing.',sections:[
    ['Measure and name both members','Mark A and B on the real pieces and take actual thickness readings in the crossing region. Identify the faces you intend to align. A request for a flush lap is incomplete until those faces are named. Enter the measured values separately even when both boards came from the same nominal stock size or were milled in the same batch.'],
    ['Choose the finished stack deliberately','The target stack is the combined thickness through the assembled crossing. It is not automatically the thinner board, thicker board or average. Select it from the design detail, then enter depth A and inspect the calculated depth B. The model rejects a recess that removes an entire member or demands a negative depth, but it does not choose a design strength.'],
    ['Inspect what remains in each part','Review each recess and each remaining section, not merely the total stack. Several depth combinations can satisfy the same overall target while leaving very different material distributions. Draw the two cross sections and their reference faces before accepting the result. Include the joint’s location along the members in the separate drawing because the planner does not locate that crossing.'],
    ['Prove the crossing in a physical pair','Prepare a representative pair using the intended tooling method. Seat the two recesses and compare the selected exterior faces to the intended alignment. Measure the actual stack rather than assuming it equals the entered target. If you change depth A after the trial, recompute B and make a new paired test; the two settings belong to one geometry record.']
  ],checks:['Actual A and B thicknesses are recorded.','The target stack and aligned faces are named.','Both remaining sections and the assembled stack are checked.'],stop:'Stop if either recess consumes a member, the face alignment is unresolved, or the physical stack differs from the accepted drawing.',faq:['Why not always remove half of each board?','Half of each gives a stack equal to the average of the two thicknesses. That may not match the intended assembly when the members differ. Use the design’s target and verify the resulting faces.'],sources:[]},
  {joint:'half-lap',slug:'repeatable-lap-shoulders',title:'Make Repeated Lap Shoulders Traceable',description:'Keep crossing widths, total notch allowance, reference ends and repeated lap positions in one coherent setup record.',intro:'The crossing member determines the notch width. The member’s own width determines the notch in its partner, so A and B must remain clearly identified.',worked:'A is 40 mm wide and B is 50 mm wide. With a total width allowance of 0.15 mm, the recess across A is 50.15 mm and the recess across B is 40.15 mm. Swapping those two widths produces plausible numbers that fit the wrong member. The planner labels both notch widths explicitly.',sections:[
    ['Map each notch to its crossing part','Place the actual pair together and label which member enters each recess. Record the widths in the crossing region. A notch in A receives B, so it uses B’s width plus the entered allowance. Write that relationship directly on the setup sheet. It is more robust than relying on a generic wide-notch or narrow-notch label.'],
    ['Use one total allowance convention','The allowance input increases the complete notch width once. Decide how the physical setup distributes that increase relative to the crossing center or shoulder. The planner does not automatically keep a centered crossing or shift a named shoulder. Put the selected location in the drawing so a width change does not silently move the visible alignment.'],
    ['Separate location from section sizing','A lap’s width and depth do not specify its distance from the end of a member. Dimension each crossing from a named reference end, or use an explicitly reviewed repeated layout. Check the final crossing against the overall part length before producing a batch. An accurate notch repeated from the wrong end can still invalidate every member.'],
    ['Inspect the first repeated set','Make a first pair and compare it to the layout record before continuing. Check both shoulders, seating depth and the intended crossing position. Keep the accepted pair available as a labeled comparator, and recheck after a stop or guide changes. If repeated members differ, identify whether the change comes from stock, location or recess width before adjusting the setup.']
  ],checks:['Each notch is labeled with its receiving member.','Allowance distribution is shown on the layout.','Repeated positions use a named reference end.'],stop:'Stop if A and B are ambiguous, a width adjustment has no location convention, or the first repeated set misses its crossing position.',faq:['Does the calculator position the lap along a rail?','It sizes the two recess sections and balances their stack. Crossing positions along the members must be dimensioned separately from their own reference ends.'],sources:[]},
  {joint:'half-lap',slug:'diagnose-a-rocking-half-lap',title:'Diagnose a Half-Lap That Rocks or Stands Proud',description:'Distinguish stack-depth errors, tapered recess floors, shoulder interference and twisted stock with a controlled dry-fit review.',intro:'A lap that stands proud does not automatically need a deeper cut everywhere. Locate the contact and compare it to the intended stack before changing both members.',worked:'An 18 mm plus 18 mm pair has two nominal 9 mm recesses and an 18 mm target stack. If the assembled crossing rocks on one corner, the arithmetic still balances. Check the floors, shoulders and stock for local contact or twist before increasing a whole recess depth and losing the intended final thickness.',sections:[
    ['Establish the actual symptom','Dry seat the clean pair and observe whether the whole crossing stands proud, one shoulder stays open or the assembly rocks diagonally. Mark the observation without forcing the parts together. Measure the stack in several places. A uniform height error and a changing corner gap suggest different causes, so do not summarize both as simply too tight.'],
    ['Check each recess independently','Compare the recess floor to the selected face datum and inspect its consistency across the crossing. Check whether either shoulder or a small unremoved corner prevents full seating. Review the actual notch width against its mating member. If one recess is wrong, identify that member and setting rather than adjusting the partner to compensate for an unknown error.'],
    ['Check stock and crossing geometry','Inspect the members for twist, bow and inconsistent thickness near the joint. Compare the physical crossing angle to the intended 90-degree model. A square notch calculation cannot correct a twisted member or an out-of-square shoulder. Use the Assembly Check workflow for the larger frame once the local joint seats, keeping the local and whole-assembly observations distinct.'],
    ['Revise one cause and test again','Record the diagnosed cause and the chosen correction before making a new trial. Recalculate the paired depths if a depth changes, then measure the new stack and face alignment. Retain the earlier observation in the notes so the accepted revision has a clear reason. Only restore the physical evidence checks after the revised pair passes the complete seating review.']
  ],checks:['The symptom is recorded before adjustment.','Each floor and shoulder has been checked separately.','The revised pair meets stack and face-alignment targets.'],stop:'Stop if closing the joint requires bending the members, the cause of rocking is still unknown, or corrections are consuming unreviewed remaining material.',faq:['Can extra clamp pressure prove that a lap fits?','No. Observe the dry contact and seating geometry first. A clamp can hide an interference or bend the members without correcting the underlying floor, shoulder or stock condition.'],sources:[]},
  {joint:'mortise-tenon',slug:'centered-tenon-shoulder-layout',title:'Read the Four Shoulders of a Centered Tenon',description:'Separate rail shoulders from receiving mortise walls and review both thickness and width axes of a centered rectangular tenon.',intro:'A centered tenon produces two pairs of rail shoulders. The receiving part has its own wall dimensions, which should not be confused with those shoulders.',worked:'A 22 × 60 mm rail with an 8 × 36 mm tenon has 7 mm cheek shoulders and 12 mm edge shoulders on each side. A total allowance of 0.1 mm on each axis gives an 8.1 × 36.1 mm mortise. In a 24 × 70 mm receiving section, its centered walls are 7.95 and 16.95 mm per side.',sections:[
    ['Name the two section axes','Label rail thickness, rail width, tenon thickness and tenon width on a section. Identify the reference face used to place the centered geometry. Do the same for the receiving part. A drawing that says only eight-millimeter tenon leaves the second axis and the shoulders unresolved. Keep the two axes visible in the notes and exported register.'],
    ['Read the rail shoulders first','Subtract tenon thickness from rail thickness and divide by two for each cheek shoulder. Repeat with widths for the two edge shoulders. These values assume exact centering and positive shoulders on all four sides. If the intended detail has a haunch, an offset or a missing shoulder, use a separate drawing rather than forcing it into this model.'],
    ['Read the receiving walls separately','The mortise uses the tenon section plus the entered total allowance on each axis. Its centered side walls come from the receiving section, not the rail section. Review the resulting walls against the full joint detail and surrounding work. A positive wall is only the model’s geometric condition; it does not establish an adequate mechanical design.'],
    ['Transfer with a consistent face convention','Mark the rail and receiving reference faces before laying out the trial. Compare the actual tool or gauge setup to the entered centered positions. Check the assembled face relationship with a physical pair, since equal center calculations on stock of different thickness can still produce a deliberate or unintended face offset. Save the face convention with the accepted revision.']
  ],checks:['Thickness and width axes are labeled.','All four rail shoulders are reviewed.','Receiving walls and assembled face positions are checked separately.'],stop:'Stop if the intended joint is offset or haunched without a separate detail, any wall disappears, or the assembled face relationship is unresolved.',faq:['Does a centered tenon make the outer faces flush?','Centering relates each section to its own middle. Different receiving and rail thicknesses can produce face offsets. Choose the intended relationship in the assembly drawing and verify it on the test pair.'],sources:[]},
  {joint:'mortise-tenon',slug:'blind-mortise-ends-and-corners',title:'Review Blind Mortise Ends and Rounded Corners',description:'Distinguish tenon length, pocket depth, blind back wall and corner compatibility before accepting a mortise-and-tenon layout.',intro:'Three depth-related numbers describe different boundaries: the tenon length, the pocket depth and the material remaining behind that pocket.',worked:'An illustrative 25 mm tenon in a 27 mm pocket has 2 mm of modeled end reserve. With 40 mm of available receiving depth, the blind back wall is 13 mm. Neither number resolves a rounded pocket end interfering with a square tenon corner, so that shape needs its own physical check.',sections:[
    ['Dimension from the seated shoulder','Measure tenon length from the shoulder that will seat against the receiving face. Measure pocket depth from that same contact plane. A protruding profile or an alternate datum can change the physical comparison. Keep the model’s entered depth aligned with the deepest pocket boundary relevant to the tenon, and describe any extra tool-point penetration separately in the shop drawing.'],
    ['Separate reserve from remaining wall','End reserve is pocket depth minus tenon length. The blind back wall is available receiving depth minus pocket depth. Increasing the pocket depth increases the first while reducing the second. Review both together instead of treating a deeper pocket as a free correction for a seating issue. The calculator rejects a pocket that reaches the entered back boundary.'],
    ['Resolve the end shape explicitly','A rectangular section does not describe the rounded ends left by some tooling. Inspect the actual pocket and tenon outline. Choose a compatible corner treatment using the intended method and check the physical result. Do not interpret extra end reserve along the length as permission for a corner interference across the section; these are different directions of fit.'],
    ['Check a clean dry seating trial','Inspect the pocket for debris and confirm the tenon reaches the seated shoulder without bottoming or corner contact. Record the actual trial dimensions and chosen corner detail. A successful trial describes that material and setup. Repeat after a change in tooling, pocket depth, stock preparation or tenon end treatment before restoring the evidence boxes in the planner.']
  ],checks:['Length and pocket depth share the seated-shoulder datum.','End reserve and blind back wall are separately reviewed.','Actual pocket and tenon corners are compatible.'],stop:'Stop if the shoulder cannot seat, the tenon bottoms unexpectedly, a corner binds, or the pocket reaches an unreviewed rear boundary.',faq:['Is the end reserve a prescribed glue reservoir?','No. It is the geometric difference between two entered lengths. The suitable joint detail and adhesive process require their own review, including the real pocket shape and clean seating trial.'],sources:[0]},
  {joint:'mortise-tenon',slug:'tenon-fit-coupon-record',title:'Keep a Tenon Fit Coupon and Revision Record',description:'Use a paired test to separate entered clearance, actual seating, adhesive suitability and design strength.',intro:'A numerical allowance is an intended difference in size. A coupon records what the real material, tools and assembly method actually produced.',worked:'An 8 mm tenon and an 8.1 mm modeled pocket differ by 0.1 mm across the thickness axis. That is a total difference, not 0.1 mm per cheek. If the real coupon does not seat, verify the measured components and contact points. Changing the input alone does not change a cutter, gauge or piece of wood.',sections:[
    ['Label a genuinely matched pair','Use representative material and identify the rail, receiving stock and reference faces. Record actual tenon section, pocket section, length and depth after preparation. Keep the two test pieces together. A loose tenon from an earlier project can be useful for comparison, but it does not prove the complete current setup or the condition of the receiving part.'],
    ['Record observations instead of a score','Note whether the shoulders seat, which faces contact, whether the joint can be assembled without forced distortion and how it comes apart for the planned process. Use the planner’s evidence boxes as a record of completed observations. They do not rank joint quality or convert a fit into a strength value. Include a short descriptive note for anything that required attention.'],
    ['Review the intended adhesive surfaces','Identify the surfaces intended to bond and the condition they will have at assembly. Check the chosen product’s substrate, application and curing instructions directly. A bare, clean coupon and a finished or contaminated production joint are different situations. Keep adhesive timing in the Glue-Up Planner so the geometric record does not become an unsupported substitute for product guidance.'],
    ['Invalidate evidence when the setup changes','A change in dimensions, cutter, gauge reference, stock condition or corner treatment needs a renewed trial. The browser clears evidence after dimension edits to make that dependency visible. Save a new revision and retain the previous record if it explains a change. A current physical test plus a traceable record is more useful than an old checked box with no setup context.']
  ],checks:['The paired coupon has stock and face identifiers.','Measured geometry and seating observations are recorded.','Adhesive surfaces and the current revision match production.'],stop:'Stop if the coupon belongs to a different setup, observations cannot be reproduced, or a proposed load claim has no separate design basis.',faq:['Does checking all three observations prove the joint is strong enough?','No. The checks document a measurement, seating and assembly review. Capacity depends on design, material, geometry, loading and execution beyond this layout model.'],sources:[0,1]},
  {joint:'dowel',slug:'map-corresponding-dowel-faces',title:'Map Dowel Centers From Corresponding Faces',description:'Label opposing faces and corresponding physical datums before transferring a paired dowel-row coordinate list.',intro:'A coordinate has meaning only with its datum. Opposing faces need corresponding marks that continue to refer to the same place after one part turns over.',worked:'A 300 mm face with four centers and 30 mm insets produces centers at 30, 110, 190 and 270 mm. The row lies 30 mm from the marked side on a 60 mm-wide face. Those numbers describe each labeled face frame; the drawing is not an instruction to turn an unmarked part over and measure from whichever edge is nearest.',sections:[
    ['Label the mating faces in assembly position','Place the two parts in their intended relationship and mark the faces that meet. Mark corresponding ends and sides while that relationship is visible. Include orientation arrows or a simple assembly sketch where helpful. Keep decorative outside faces distinct from the boring reference faces so the parts can be returned to the same frame after handling.'],
    ['Define the coordinate frame in words','Write that center distance starts at the marked end and row offset starts at the marked side. The tool models two equal, opposing rectangular faces with matching local coordinates. It does not infer a jig transformation for a corner or edge-to-face joint. For another configuration, prepare its dedicated drawing and follow the jig manufacturer’s setup rather than reinterpreting these coordinates.'],
    ['Compare the physical reference method','Review how the actual jig registers against a face, end and side. Manufacturer systems have specific reference marks, spacers and configuration steps. Confirm that the entered offset can be produced by the chosen setup and that the marks remain visible after clamping. A coordinate table does not establish a jig’s available range or certify a particular configuration.'],
    ['Prove the correspondence with a test pair','Use the intended configuration on matched scraps and check whether the corresponding centers and selected exterior faces align in assembly. Record the successful orientation in the notes or a photo kept with the job. Recheck after changing a spacer, fence reference or face selection. Preserve the paired test even if every coordinate in the digital record remains unchanged.']
  ],checks:['Mating faces and corresponding ends are marked.','The row offset uses a named physical side.','The actual jig configuration has passed a paired test.'],stop:'Stop if a turnover changes the intended datum, a jig reference is unclear, or the real joint is outside the equal-face configuration.',faq:['Can this row map replace an edge-to-face jig manual?','No. This model maps two identical opposing faces. A manufacturer’s corner or edge-to-face configuration can require different datums, spacers, offsets and depth choices.'],sources:[2]},
  {joint:'dowel',slug:'dowel-bore-depth-and-tip',title:'Separate Dowel Bore Depth From Drill-Tip Penetration',description:'Check usable cylindrical socket length, drill-point reach, remaining outer skin and paired dowel-length reserve.',intro:'The deepest point of a drill is not necessarily the full-diameter bottom of its hole. A depth review should keep those two measurements separate.',worked:'Each 18 mm-thick part has a 12 mm full-diameter bore plus 2 mm of additional point penetration. The deepest point reaches 14 mm, leaving 4 mm of outer skin. A 22 mm dowel in the two cylindrical bores has 2 mm of total modeled length reserve: 12 + 12 − 22.',sections:[
    ['Measure the full-diameter socket','Determine the usable cylindrical depth of the trial hole with the actual bit and setup. Enter that as full-diameter bore depth. Do not assume a stop-collar distance equals the usable socket without inspecting the result. The collar, guide thickness, bit geometry and reference surface all contribute to the physical drilling arrangement and remain specific to the equipment.'],
    ['Account for the deepest point','Enter additional point penetration beyond the cylindrical depth. The model adds it once per part when checking remaining skin. Inspect the actual bit because a small center point and a more extended profile can produce different deepest reaches. If the bit or stop arrangement changes, repeat the depth trial in representative material rather than transferring an old number without review.'],
    ['Compare the dowel to both sockets','The pair’s modeled usable depth is twice the entered cylindrical depth because this configuration uses equal parts and equal bores. Subtract the actual dowel length to see the total reserve. This result does not distribute the dowel between parts or guarantee seating. Check engagement, debris, dowel end shape and the assembly sequence with the physical pair.'],
    ['Inspect the outer faces and dry fit','Examine the trial for breakout, bulging or visible surface damage. Dry assemble with the intended dowel and confirm the faces seat. Keep the remaining-skin result as a recorded dimension rather than a universal minimum recommendation. The planner blocks zero or negative skin; deciding whether the positive remainder suits the design remains part of the physical and design review.']
  ],checks:['Full-diameter depth was measured in a trial hole.','Additional point reach is recorded.','The real dowel seats and the outer faces remain intact.'],stop:'Stop if the deepest point reaches the outer face, the dowel exceeds the usable pair depth, or the depth convention cannot be reproduced.',faq:['Why not use total tip depth to calculate dowel reserve?','The narrow point cavity may not accept the full dowel diameter. The model uses cylindrical socket depth for dowel length and total point penetration for the outer-skin check.'],sources:[2]},
  {joint:'dowel',slug:'dowel-row-spacing-and-drift',title:'Check Dowel Row Spacing and Repeated-Index Drift',description:'Compute equally spaced centers from both end insets and verify physical indexing without assuming a coordinate list prevents cumulative error.',intro:'Equal spacing in a calculation does not ensure equal spacing in a drilled row. The reference and indexing method still need a test over the full pattern.',worked:'Four centers across a 300 mm face with 30 mm insets leave 240 mm between the first and last centers. Dividing by three gaps gives an 80 mm pitch. Using four as the divisor would give 60 mm and place the last center at 210 mm, missing the far inset by 60 mm.',sections:[
    ['Count gaps instead of only holes','For a row with at least two holes, the number of equal gaps is one fewer than the number of centers. Review the first center, computed pitch and last center together. The planner fixes equal insets at both ends, so changing the count changes pitch. It does not preserve an arbitrary manufacturer index interval while also forcing the final inset.'],
    ['Keep whole hole circles inside the face','The inset locates a center, not the rim of a hole. Compare the radius to both end insets and to the row’s side position. The model rejects overlapping neighboring circles and circles outside the entered face. These geometric checks do not prescribe suitable edge distance or dowel count for a mechanical design; choose those from the intended joint detail.'],
    ['Review the actual indexing method','Compare the calculated pitch to the available jig guides, indexing accessories or measured transfer method. If the pattern cannot be produced by that system, revise the layout or use an appropriate documented configuration. Do not round each coordinate independently and assume the pair remains equivalent. Record the precision and physical reference used to transfer the complete pattern.'],
    ['Check the first, middle and final positions','Inspect a representative full row, including the far endpoint after all repeated indexing steps. A good first hole does not reveal error accumulated farther along the pattern. Compare corresponding trial faces before drilling the production pair. If the final position drifts, review references and indexing before moving only the last hole, which would break the equal-spacing model.']
  ],checks:['The pitch uses count minus one gaps.','Hole rims stay within the modeled face.','A full trial row matches at its first, middle and last positions.'],stop:'Stop if the required pitch is unsupported by the setup, repeated indexing drifts, or rounded transfers no longer describe the same paired pattern.',faq:['Can I keep a fixed pitch and choose any count?','This planner derives pitch from the span, equal end insets and count. A fixed-pitch layout is a different constraint set; resolve its final inset explicitly instead of silently changing this result.'],sources:[2]},
  {joint:'box',slug:'box-joint-count-and-remainder',title:'Count Box-Joint Intervals and Expose the Remainder',description:'Distinguish intervals, tabs and sockets, inspect the last partial interval and keep the complementary A/B phase visible.',intro:'A span rarely promises an exact number of fingers at your preferred width. Show the final interval before deciding how the board edges should look.',worked:'A 123 mm span divided into nominal 10 mm intervals produces twelve full intervals plus one 3 mm interval. Panel A keeps the first interval and panel B removes it; each following interval swaps that role. The planner retains the 3 mm end interval instead of silently trimming the panel or stretching every finger.',sections:[
    ['Define what the count represents','The result counts intervals across the shared span, including both material tabs and removed sockets. It does not count only the fingers on one panel. Label A and B before reading the register: A keeps the first interval and B receives it. The phase then alternates at every nominal boundary, and the final edge depends on that count.'],
    ['Inspect the exact endpoint','Compare the span to whole multiples of the entered interval width. When there is a remainder, read its actual width in the final row. Small floating-point differences are treated as exact multiples within the model’s stated numerical tolerance, while a real dimensional remainder stays visible. Measure the prepared board width before accepting the end pattern for a production box.'],
    ['Choose a deliberate edge treatment','Decide whether the partial end interval belongs in the design. Alternatives such as changing the span, changing the nominal width or selecting a different jig system need a fresh layout. The calculator does not make that design choice. Draw the top and bottom edge conditions of both panels so a visually acceptable face is not paired with an unintended weak or awkward end detail.'],
    ['Compare the map to the cutting system','The interval map is a nominal boundary record. A jig may use fixed fingers, a particular cutter or fit adjustments that do not accept an arbitrary width. Check the actual system’s instructions and make a trial pair. Keep kerf and fit compensation outside this nominal map so the dimension list is not mistaken for a sequence of saw or router moves.']
  ],checks:['Intervals are distinguished from tabs on one panel.','The final interval is measured and accepted.','A/B phase matches the intended top and bottom edges.'],stop:'Stop if a partial interval has no accepted detail, the jig cannot produce the layout, or a boundary list is being used as an unreviewed cutting sequence.',faq:['Does the planner automatically remove a narrow last finger?','No. It preserves the entered span and exposes the remaining interval. Change the design inputs deliberately and review both panel phases before adopting a different endpoint.'],sources:[3]},
  {joint:'box',slug:'box-joint-fit-and-four-corners',title:'Prove Box-Joint Fit Through All Four Corners',description:'Move from a scrap fit pair to a labeled dry box while keeping jig compensation, panel phase and assembly order separate.',intro:'One successful corner is an important test, but a complete box has a phase, orientation and assembly problem at every corner.',worked:'A 120 mm span at 10 mm nominal intervals has twelve intervals. The map alternates A tabs with B sockets. Before producing a box, label the front, back, left and right parts and check which complementary end meets at each corner. A correctly sized pattern can still be cut on the wrong face or in the wrong phase.',sections:[
    ['Begin with the actual fit procedure','Use the cutting system’s documented scrap-test and fit-adjustment method. The planner does not calculate kerf or compensation from a nominal interval width. Keep the accepted scraps with the setup and identify their reference faces. If a cutter, bushing, indexing component or stock preparation changes, renew the trial before assuming the previous fit remains representative.'],
    ['Label the whole panel set','Draw the assembled box from above and label each panel and both of its ends. Mark the relevant face and top edge on the real parts. Identify where A-like and B-like patterns meet. A common naming convention through drawing, coupon and production pieces makes it possible to inspect the planned pairing before a mistaken phase is repeated across the batch.'],
    ['Dry assemble every corner together','Fit the full set without adhesive and inspect shoulder seating, top-edge relationship and overall squareness. A single corner pair cannot reveal whether the final corner closes or whether a bottom panel obstructs the insertion path. Include any grooves, bottoms or dividers that affect the real assembly. Record the order that works and the places needing attention.'],
    ['Carry the sequence into the glue-up plan','Use the successful dry assembly to assign a realistic batch and staging order. Keep clamps, pads and access in the rehearsal. Review adhesive instructions for the selected product and surfaces rather than treating a nominal tab area as a glue schedule. Mark the planner’s observations only after the full sequence has been tested with the current parts and setup.']
  ],checks:['The actual cutting system passes its scrap fit test.','Every panel end has a face and phase label.','All four corners and the bottom pass one complete dry assembly.'],stop:'Stop if the last corner cannot close, a panel phase is ambiguous, or the assembly sequence exists only as an untested assumption.',faq:['Does the A/B map tell me how to index my jig?','It describes complementary material intervals only. Jig indexing, cutter compensation and fit adjustments depend on the specific cutting system and its instructions.'],sources:[3]},
  {joint:'box',slug:'box-joint-depth-unequal-panels',title:'Set Box-Joint Depths for Unequal Panels',description:'Relate each panel’s socket depth to its mating thickness, review trimming allowance and avoid confusing depth with finger fit.',intro:'The cut in one panel receives the thickness of the other. Unequal stock therefore needs two depth values even when both panels share one interval map.',worked:'Panel A is 12 mm thick and B is 12.1 mm. With an illustrative 0.2 mm extra depth, A’s cut depth is 12.3 mm and B’s is 12.2 mm. The extra depth permits an intended proud end in the idealized corner. It does not widen a socket or provide a fit adjustment across the fingers.',sections:[
    ['Measure each mating panel group','Take actual thickness readings near the ends that will form the corners. Keep A and B groups identified if their thicknesses differ. The depth across A is based on B’s thickness, and the depth across B is based on A’s. A single nominal thickness can hide a small mismatch that becomes visible after the complete corner is seated.'],
    ['Choose extra depth as a finishing decision','The extra-depth input adds the same entered amount to both mating-depth calculations. It is an explicit design and cleanup choice, not a required universal setting. Consider the intended final corner and available trimming method. Preserve the planned finished dimensions in the project drawing so removing proud ends does not unexpectedly change a visible edge or adjacent detail.'],
    ['Keep depth separate from width fit','A deeper socket changes how far a tab can enter relative to the mating thickness. It does not correct a tab that binds across its width or a socket widened by the wrong cutter compensation. Diagnose those issues using the actual fit-test method. Record depth and interval-width changes separately so a successful adjustment can be reproduced without mixing two different causes.'],
    ['Inspect the seated corner before cleanup','Dry seat the representative pair and compare shoulder contact and proud-end distribution on both faces. Measure the real result before trimming hides the original contact conditions. Then review the complete panel set and any bottom groove affected by the depth. Update the revision and coupon if thickness changes between batches or after additional stock preparation.']
  ],checks:['A and B actual thicknesses are separately recorded.','Each depth uses the mating panel thickness.','The seated corner is inspected before trimming.'],stop:'Stop if one depth is being reused despite unequal stock, extra depth is being used to mask a width-fit problem, or trimming has no accepted final-size plan.',faq:['Is the extra depth the same as kerf allowance?','No. Extra depth acts into the panel end. Kerf and fit compensation act on the cut boundaries across the span and require the cutting system’s own setup and test method.'],sources:[3]}
];

export const chapterNotes = {
  dado:{question:'Will the actual shelf edge enter both housings?',decision:'Start with measured mating stock, use one total width allowance and transfer both walls from a named datum.',steps:['Measure the entering edge at several points.','Review the housing width, floor and far wall.','Compare the opposing panel references.','Test a representative strip, then the complete case.'],example:'An entered 17.8 mm edge + 0.15 mm total allowance produces a 17.95 mm housing. The example uses a 6 mm depth in an 18 mm host, leaving 12 mm. These are demonstration inputs, not a prescribed cabinet detail.'},
  rabbet:{question:'Which wall locates the panel, and what remains behind the seat?',decision:'Draw the entering edge in section before entering width and depth. Keep panel outline, movement and retention in the assembly drawing.',steps:['Name the two section axes and open edge.','Measure the entering panel section.','Review shoulder coordinate and remaining lip.','Rehearse installation, retention and finish boundaries.'],example:'The example 6 mm entering edge plus 0.15 mm total allowance needs a 6.15 mm seat. Across a 180 mm host span, the shoulder is 173.85 mm from the opposite datum. An entered 9 mm recess leaves 9 mm of the 18 mm host.'},
  'half-lap':{question:'Do the two recesses produce the intended stack and face alignment?',decision:'Choose the finished combined thickness explicitly. Review both remaining sections and the physical crossing rather than assuming half-depth cuts.',steps:['Measure both members and mark the aligned faces.','Enter target stack and recess A.','Review calculated recess B and cross-member notch widths.','Test stack, seating and face alignment as a pair.'],example:'With 18 mm members, an 18 mm target stack and a 9 mm recess in A, B also needs 9 mm. The 40 and 50 mm crossing widths become 40.15 and 50.15 mm receiving notches under the example total allowance.'},
  'mortise-tenon':{question:'Can the shoulder seat while all receiving walls remain?',decision:'Track the rail’s four shoulders, the mortise’s separate wall dimensions and the depth reserve. Resolve corner shape in the physical trial.',steps:['Label thickness and width axes on both parts.','Review centered rail shoulders and receiving walls.','Compare tenon length, pocket depth and back wall.','Prove compatible corners and clean shoulder seating.'],example:'The 22 × 60 mm sample rail has an 8 × 36 × 25 mm tenon. The centered cheek shoulders are 7 mm; edge shoulders are 12 mm. An 8.1 × 36.1 mm mortise at 27 mm depth leaves 2 mm of modeled end reserve.'},
  dowel:{question:'Do matching face frames produce matching centers and usable bores?',decision:'This planner covers two identical opposing faces. Enter cylindrical socket depth and additional point penetration separately, then prove the actual reference method.',steps:['Mark corresponding mating ends and sides.','Set count and equal center insets.','Review pitch, full circles, tip reach and length reserve.','Drill and assemble a complete trial row.'],example:'Four holes across 300 mm with 30 mm center insets give an 80 mm pitch. Two 12 mm cylindrical bores receive the example 22 mm dowel with 2 mm total reserve. In 18 mm stock, an extra 2 mm point leaves 4 mm of modeled outer skin.'},
  box:{question:'Are both end patterns intentional and all corner phases compatible?',decision:'Inspect the complete complementary interval map, including a partial last interval. Establish cutting compensation with the actual jig’s own fit-test method.',steps:['Measure the shared span and label A/B panel groups.','Choose a nominal interval and inspect the endpoint.','Review separate cut depths for the two mating thicknesses.','Test scraps, then dry assemble all four corners.'],example:'A 120 mm span at 10 mm intervals gives twelve complete intervals. With 12 and 12.1 mm panel thicknesses and an illustrative 0.2 mm extra depth, A’s sockets are 12.3 mm deep and B’s are 12.2 mm deep.'}
};
